"At the beginning of Surah al-Haqqah, the word \"al-Haqqah,\" which is one of the names of the Day of Resurrection, is dwelt upon. Twelve different views have been transmitted regarding the meaning of this word. Among these are interpretations such as \"the Hour whose occurrence is inevitable,\" \"that which reveals the reality of things,\" and \"the Day on which every deed is requited.\" However, all of them emphasize that al-Haqqah cannot be fully known through reason and contemplation, but can only be known through Allah's notification.\n\nThe question \"What is that Haqqah?\" is asked to demonstrate the magnitude and horror of the event. This question emphasizes the dreadfulness of al-Haqqah and expresses that it can only be made known by Allah, beyond the grasp of human intellect. For this reason, since the disbelievers cannot comprehend it with their intellects, they attempt to deny it.\n\nThe past nations that denied al-Haqqah are described. The peoples of Thamud and 'Ad denied \"the striking calamity (al-Qari'ah),\" that is, the tremendous event that strikes people. Thamud was destroyed by al-Taghiyah (the overwhelming cataclysm); as for 'Ad, they were annihilated by rih sarsar (a furiously howling, intensely cold gale). This storm was \"husum,\" that is, an ill-omened wind that cut off their roots.\n\nThose before Pharaoh and the people of Lot were punished in a similar manner. All of them denied the verses of Allah through wrongdoing (deliberately committing sin). Allah punished them with an increasingly overwhelming seizure. However, during the time of the Flood of Noah, He protected those who believed by carrying them in the floating Ark.\n\nThese events serve as a reminder and a lesson for people. Allah desires that these reminders be listened to and learned by attentive ears and that their requirements be fulfilled. Following this, the description of the Great Resurrection begins: the Trumpet (Sur) will be blown, the earth and mountains will be lifted and crushed against each other, and on that Day the Inevitable Event (al-Waqi'ah, the Resurrection) will take place.\n\nDifferent interpretations have been made regarding the condition of the earth and the mountains. According to one view, the mountains fly into the air and turn into dust and smoke, and the earth becomes completely flat. According to another view, the earth breaks apart earlier, while the mountains melt and flow away. A third view is that both perish simultaneously and equally. In any case, on that Day everything undergoes transformation in order to appear before Allah.\n\nOn that Day, the heaven will be split apart with clouds, and the angels will descend in succession. The sun and the moon will be eclipsed, the stars will disappear, and the sky will take on a mournful and sorrowful state. (Al-Haqqah, 16)\n\nIn the continuation of the verse, it is stated that the angels are lined up in ranks at the split edges of the heaven. The term 'malak' (angel) mentioned here is a generic noun referring to all angels. Scholars have debated the difference between 'malak' (angel) and 'mala'ikah' (angels); some have said that 'malak' is more comprehensive, while others have stated that 'malak' is more specific than 'mala'ikah.' In conclusion, the use of 'malak' in the verse points to the angels charged with a special duty on that Day. (Al-Haqqah, 17)\n\nOn that Day, eight angels will bear the Throne ('Arsh). There are various narrations regarding the number, nature, and identity of these eight. In a hadith, it is reported that they are four today and will be eight on the Day of Resurrection. Since we do not possess definitive knowledge on this matter, the most correct approach is to say 'Allah knows best' and not exceed the bounds of what has been transmitted. (Al-Haqqah, 17)\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, everyone will be presented before Allah to give account. This presentation is such an exposition that nothing hidden will remain. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) informed that people will be presented three times: in the first two, there will be dispute and presentation of excuses, and in the third, the record books of deeds will fly into hands. (Al-Haqqah, 18)\n\nThe believer who is given his book in his right hand says with joy, 'Here, read my book; for indeed, I knew that I would meet my account.' He leads a pleasing life in an elevated Paradise; its fruits hanging low, he eats and drinks as he pleases. These blessings are the reward for what they sent forth in the cause of Allah while in the world. (Al-Haqqah, 19-24)\n\nIslaf is to offer and present beforehand with the return coming on credit; in commerce, it is called salaf or salam. In this verse, the expression \"what you advanced\" alludes to this transaction, to giving a goodly loan to Allah, and to the benefit derived from serving an elder. And this can be done not during times of hardship, but in unoccupied and opportune days, especially in the prime of youth. According to a narration, on that Day Allah will proclaim: \"O My friends! In the world, I often looked upon you while your lips were parched with thirst in My way, your eyes sunken, and your bellies clinging to your loins. Today, eat and drink in return for what you offered and advanced in those bygone days.\"\n\nThe one who is given his book in his left hand says: \"Ah, would that that first death had been the end of it all!\" This wishing to die once more is not due to the pain of death, but because of the horror of the catastrophe he witnesses. He neither dies nor lives anymore; his wealth avails him nothing, and his dominion vanishes. This regret is for those who relied on power and wealth in this world and miscalculated their account. Indeed, Fana Khusraw, known by the title 'Adud al-Dawlah, went mad while boasting of being \"ghallab al-qadar\" (the overcomer of destiny) and constantly repeated these verses as he gave up his soul; in the end, he was crushed beneath the foot of destiny.\n\nAllah the Almighty commands the angels of punishment (zabaniyah): \"Seize him, shackle him, then cast him into the Blazing Fire (Jahim), then bind him in a chain seventy cubits long.\" Seventy cubits may be a metonym for great quantity, or it may indicate that it is far-fetched for those who do not believe until the age of seventy to ever reform. The reason for this punishment is two things: first, that person did not believe in Allah the Almighty; second, he did not encourage the feeding of the destitute. He did not look after the poor, did not offer aid, and was not even humble enough to be present at a meal with the poor.\n\nOn that Day, there will be for him neither a hamim (a close friend or cool water) nor any food, except for ghislin (a foul substance like purulent discharge). None eats this except the great sinners whose sole preoccupation is committing errors and crimes, especially those who associate partners with Allah. This recalls the end of deniers such as Pharaoh and the people of Lot.\n\nThen Allah swears by what you see and what you do not see, declaring that the Quran is the word brought by an honorable messenger. Here, attention is drawn to the error of positivists and sophists; believing only in what is seen and denying what is unseen is a grave mistake. Yet experience demonstrates that what lies beyond the visible also exists. Knowledge obtained through reliable transmission, especially revelation coming from a trustworthy messenger, conveys realities that lie beyond reason and empirical experience.\n\nThe Quran is not the word of a poet; little do you believe. Nor is it the word of a soothsayer; little do you reflect! It is a revelation sent down from the Lord of the worlds. If the Prophet had attempted to fabricate any sayings falsely attributed to Allah, Allah would have seized him by the right hand and severed his aorta; then none of you could have shielded him.\n\nThe Quran is an unforgettable reminder for the righteous (people of taqwa). Nevertheless, Allah knows that there are deniers among you. It is indeed a source of regret for the disbelievers; truly, it is knowledge beyond any doubt. So glorify the magnificent name of your Lord.\n\nIn the 43rd verse of Surah al-Haqqah, Allah states that the Quran has been sent down from Allah the Almighty, the Lord of all the worlds. In previous verses, it was said, \"It is the word of an honorable messenger\"; here, it is emphasized that both the meaning and the words of the Quran were revealed by Allah. Thus, the misconception that it is the messenger's own speech is prevented.\n\nIn verses 44–45, it is stated that if the messenger had fabricated lies in the name of Allah, Allah would seize and punish him. The word 'yamin' can be interpreted either as the oaths sworn by the prophet or as the power of Allah. In any case, it is related that the messenger who swears falsely would be disgraced.\n\nVerses 46–47 explain the extent of this punishment: Allah cuts the aorta (or spinal cord) of the liar, meaning He destroys him. At that point, no one can save him. Yet the reality is that the messenger is a trustworthy prophet, far removed from falsehood, and the Quran has truly been sent down from Allah.\n\nVerses 48–50 touch upon the wisdoms of the Quran: It is an admonition and a reminder for the people of taqwa (the God-conscious). However, there are deniers among us; they will face their punishment. The Quran is a source of bitter regret for the disbelievers; on the Day of Resurrection, when they see the reward of the people of taqwa, they will feel boundless remorse.\n\nVerse 51 declares that the Quran is haqqu'l-yaqin (absolute certainty), meaning there is no doubt whatsoever about its reality. It possesses a higher degree of certainty than 'ilmu'l-yaqin (certainty by knowledge) and 'aynu'l-yaqin (certainty by sight). In the final verse, the messenger is commanded to glorify the exalted name of his Lord."